EXT. WAGON CIRCLE - DUSK

Six wagons drawn into a ring on a wind-scoured flat. A fire burns low in the center. The sky is bruise-colored. Far off, a coyote starts and stops.

The company is at supper. WALT BREDEN, 50s, the wagon master, sits on an upturned barrel with a tin plate on his knee. His wife DOROTHEA, 40s, stirs the pot and slaps a fly off her arm. Their son HENRY, 13, whittles a stick to nothing. The scout LUIS ARRIAGA, 30s, squats by the fire with a coffee pot, pouring into mismatched cups for the others. The widow CATHERINE MULDOON, 60s, rocks in a canvas chair, knitting, her needles clicking. And the stranger they picked up two days back, a heavy-set man with a limp who calls himself ABEL, 40s, sits a little way off with his plate untouched.

Nobody says much. The knitting needles click.

WALT
(to everyone)
We make the river by Thursday if the oxen hold.

LUIS
They'll hold. It's the wheels I'd worry about.

DOROTHEA
(to Henry)
Eat your beans.

HENRY
I'm not hungry.

DOROTHEA
Eat them anyway.

Walt sets his plate aside, rises, and walks the length of the circle with his thumbs in his belt, checking each wagon's brake. Luis fills his own cup. Catherine knits. Henry flips the stick into the fire. Dorothea snatches the plate from his hands and hands it to Abel instead, who accepts it with a nod and begins to eat. Walt, at the far wagon, stops and mutters to himself.

WALT
(muttered)
Third spoke's cracked.

LUIS
(from the fire)
What's that?

WALT
Nothing.

He kneels to inspect it. Catherine's needles stop.

CATHERINE
(to no one in particular)
There's a rider.

Everyone turns. Far across the flat, a single horseman crests a low rise and halts, black against the afterglow.

HENRY
(whispering)
Is it Indians?

DOROTHEA
Hush.

LUIS
(low, to Walt)
He's alone.

WALT
(rising)
Then he's no trouble. Henry, fetch the rifle.

HENRY
Yes, sir.

He scrambles to the wagon, rummages, returns with it. Walt takes it, checks the chamber, and lays it across his arm. Abel has stopped eating. He has set his plate on the ground and is looking, not at the rider, but at Luis, who is looking back.

Catherine resumes her knitting. The rider does not move. The fire pops.

ABEL
(to himself)
Told him not to follow.

Nobody hears him but Luis.
